<BaseLayout title="Taiyang — Sun Point (Extra Point)" description="Everything you need to self-treat Taiyang — exact location, when to use it, a step-by-step routine, symptom-based point combinations, and expected results." breadcrumbs={[{ label: "Home", href: "/" }, { label: "Points", href: "/points/" }, { label: "Taiyang", href: "/points/taiyang-acupressure-point/" }]} datePublished="2026-08-19" dateModified="2026-08-19" articleSchema={true}>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-12 lg:px-6">
    <h1 class="mb-4">Taiyang — Sun Point</h1>
    <p class="text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 text-lg mb-8">Taiyang, the Sun point, is one of the most widely used extra points of the head and face. Resting in the hollow of the temple, it is a go-to point for tension headaches, migraines, eye strain and dry eyes, and the facial tightness that builds up during long screen days.</p>

    <section class="mb-8">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">At a Glance</h2>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Meridian</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Extra point (not on a meridian)</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Body region</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Temple, one finger width behind the midpoint between the eyebrow tip and outer eye corner</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Best for</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Tension headaches, migraines, eye strain, dry eyes, toothache, facial tension</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Difficulty</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Very easy to find and press — both temples with your own fingers</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Hold time</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">1–2 minutes, gentle pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Frequency</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">As needed during screen breaks, or daily for tension relief</td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Pairs well with</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300"><a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB20 (Fengchi)</a>, <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">Yintang</a>, <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4 (Hegu)</a></td></tr>
            <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-40">Skip if</th><td class="px-4 py-2.5 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Recent eye surgery, head or face trauma, or broken skin at the temple — and always press gently (see Precautions)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Why Taiyang Matters</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Taiyang is an extra point, which means it does not belong to any of the fourteen meridians — but it is one of the most commonly pressed points on the entire head and face. It sits directly over the temporalis muscle, the fan of muscle on the side of the head that clenches when you grind your teeth, squint at a screen, or hold stress in your jaw. That direct placement is why it responds so reliably to simple finger massage: you can feel the tension that lives in that area release almost immediately.</p>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed">Because it does not require tracing a meridian path, Taiyang is easy to learn and to apply anytime — at a desk, during a long meeting, or before bed. Together with Yintang and GB20, it anchors our head and face routines for screen fatigue and tension-type headache, and it is often the first point taught to beginners because the position is so obvious once you feel the temple hollow.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Where Is This Point Located?</h2>
    <img src="/images/taiyang-location.webp" alt="Taiyang acupressure point location in the hollow of the temple" class="w-full max-w-sm mx-auto rounded-xl shadow-sm" width="474" height="312" loading="lazy">
    <p class="mb-3">Taiyang lies in the depression at the temple, about one finger width (roughly 1 cun) posterior to the midpoint of the line connecting the outer tip of the eyebrow and the outer corner of the eye (the outer canthus). Close your eyes, rest your index and middle fingers on the temple, and press gently — you will feel a soft hollow between the edge of the eye socket and the temple bone. That hollow is the point.</p>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3"><strong>Three quick confirmations you have the right spot:</strong> (1) it is the soft hollow just behind the midpoint between the eyebrow tip and the outer eye corner; (2) light pressure produces a distinctly tender or "wired" sensation that the surrounding skin does not; (3) the tenderness usually tracks your tension level — the point feels more sensitive on stressed, screen-heavy days.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">When to Use Taiyang (and When Not)</h2>
    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-forest-200 bg-forest-50 dark:border-forest-900/40 dark:bg-forest-900/20 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Use it when</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• A tension headache or migraine is building at the temple</li>
          <li>• Your eyes feel strained, tired, or dry after long screen time</li>
          <li>• Jaw clenching or facial tightness is building up</li>
          <li>• You want a simple wind-down before sleep or during a break</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-stone-50 dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800/50 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-2 text-sm">Hold off if</h3>
        <ul class="space-y-1.5 text-sm text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
          <li>• You recently had eye surgery, or any head or face trauma</li>
          <li>• The skin at the temple is broken, bruised, or irritated</li>
          <li>• Pressure causes dizziness, blurred vision, or a worsening headache</li>
          <li>• The headache is sudden, severe, or the worst you have ever had</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Use the <em>Hold off</em> column as a safety filter: Taiyang is a comfort practice for tension and eye strain, not a treatment for a sudden or severe headache — and pressure here must always stay gentle.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Are the Benefits?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li><strong>Tension headache:</strong> Taiyang is a primary point for the band-like pressure headaches that concentrate at the temples, and it is also used for migraine-related head pain.</li>
      <li><strong>Eye strain:</strong> It relieves tired, strained eyes, dry eyes, and visual fatigue from computer, phone, or driving use.</li>
      <li><strong>Facial tension:</strong> It eases tightness in the temporalis muscle, which helps with jaw clenching and facial stress.</li>
      <li><strong>Toothache:</strong> In traditional practice it is used for pain in the upper jaw and face, alongside dental care.</li>
      <li><strong>Relaxation:</strong> Slow circular massage at the temples is a simple, effective way to wind down a racing head before sleep.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">How Do You Use This Point?</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">Use the pads of your index and middle fingers, press into the temple hollow with a light-to-moderate touch, and work slow clockwise circles while breathing deeply. The muscle layer here is thin, so gentle pressure goes a long way — more is not better at the temple.</p>
    <h3 class="mb-2 text-lg font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Step-by-step technique</h3>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Sit comfortably with your back supported and close your eyes.</li>
      <li>Place the pads of your index and middle fingers on the hollow of each temple.</li>
      <li>Apply gentle to moderate pressure and make slow, small clockwise circles.</li>
      <li>Breathe deeply for 1–2 minutes, keeping the circles small and the pressure even.</li>
      <li>Ease the pressure gradually, then rest with your eyes closed for a moment before continuing your day.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-3 text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed"><strong>Common mistake to avoid:</strong> pressing hard or fast at the temple. The skin is thin and the area sits close to the eye — the target is a gentle, even, achy relief, not force. If you feel dizzy, see spots, or the headache worsens, stop immediately and seek medical advice.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Combine Taiyang with These Points</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">A head-and-shoulders routine works better than a single point. Pick the row closest to your situation and work the points in the order shown, 1–2 minutes each, with gentle pressure.</p>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 overflow-hidden">
      <table class="w-full text-sm">
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-stone-100 dark:divide-stone-800">
          <tr><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 w-44">Goal / symptom</th><th class="text-left px-4 py-2.5 font-semibold text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">Routine (in order)</th></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Tension headache at the temples</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Taiyang → <a href="/points/gb20-fengchi-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">GB20</a> → <a href="/points/li4-hegu-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LI4</a>, 60–90s each, in a quiet space</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Screen fatigue and eye strain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Taiyang → <a href="/points/yintang-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">Yintang</a>, 60s each, during every screen break</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Migraine-related head pain</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">GB20 → Taiyang → Yintang, holding each 90s, with a dark, quiet room and fluids</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 align-top text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Stress and jaw clenching</td><td class="px-4 py-3 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">Taiyang → Yintang → <a href="/points/lv3-taichong-acupressure-point/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">LV3</a>, 60s each, as a nightly wind-down</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400">For a fuller program, see the <a href="/body/face-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">face acupressure points</a> and <a href="/body/head-acupressure-points/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">head acupressure points</a> body guides, and our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-migraine/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for migraine</a> page.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What to Expect</h2>
    <div class="space-y-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">First session (today)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">The temple is usually tender, and you may feel the tension in the temporalis muscle ease within a minute or two of slow circular pressure. Many people feel a distinct "unclenching" around the eyes and jaw. Do not expect a serious headache to vanish after one press.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Days 3–7 (consistent daily practice)</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">With regular use during screen breaks and at the first sign of tension, the temple often feels less chronically tight and headaches may build less often. This matches the pattern in studies of acupressure for tension-type headache, where benefits tend to appear after several days of regular practice.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-900 p-4">
        <h3 class="font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-1 text-sm">Weeks 2–4</h3>
        <p class="text-sm text-stone-600 dark:text-stone-300">For recurring headaches, Taiyang works best alongside eye breaks, posture, hydration, and sleep. If headaches are frequent, severe, or worsening, or if you notice vision changes, see a clinician — a sudden or "worst ever" headache needs emergency care, not more pressure.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">What Does the Research Say?</h2>
    <p class="mb-3">Evidence on acupressure for headache and pain has grown in recent years. Systematic reviews and randomized controlled trials have examined acupressure as an adjunct for headache conditions, with results that are generally promising but limited in quality. Meta-analyses of acupressure for tension-type headache tend to show a positive direction of effect, and manual pressure on head and face extra points such as Taiyang appears in many multi-point self-care protocols — though the included studies are often small and their methodological quality varies considerably.</p>
    <p class="mb-3">Researchers consistently note that acupressure trials are hard to blind, because the treatment is physical and participants can usually tell whether they are being treated. This makes it difficult to separate the specific effects of a particular point such as Taiyang from the general benefits of touch, pressure, and relaxation. The overall picture — which we discuss in detail on our <a href="/research/does-acupressure-work/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">does acupressure work?</a> review — is one of encouraging signals alongside a clear need for larger, higher-quality studies before firm conclusions can be drawn. For headaches specifically, our <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-headache/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for headache</a> and <a href="/conditions/acupressure-for-migraine/" class="text-forest-600 hover:text-forest-500 dark:text-forest-300">acupressure for migraine</a> pages summarize the strongest available evidence.</p>

    <EvidenceBox refs={["hsieh-2010"]} note="The randomized trial above is the closest direct evidence we cite: it tested acupressure for headache, the condition Taiyang is most used for. It did not study Taiyang as a single point." />

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Are There Safety Concerns?</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 mb-3 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
      <li>Use very light pressure — the skin at the temple is thin, the muscle is delicate, and the area sits close to the eye.</li>
      <li>Do not press if the skin is broken, bruised, irritated, or has a rash, sore, or pimple at the temple.</li>
      <li>Use extreme caution, or avoid the point entirely, after recent eye surgery, orbital fracture, or head or face trauma — consult a doctor first.</li>
      <li>Stop immediately if you feel dizziness, blurred vision, a new or worsening headache, or pain that radiates behind the eye.</li>
    </ul>

    </section><section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Red Flags — See a Professional</h2>
    <div class="rounded-xl border border-red-200 bg-red-50 dark:border-red-900/40 dark:bg-red-900/20 p-4 text-sm">
      <p class="text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300 mb-2">Stop self-treatment and see a doctor if you notice any of the following:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 text-stone-700 dark:text-stone-300">
        <li>A headache that is sudden, severe, or the worst you have ever had</li>
        <li>Headache with vision changes, confusion, weakness, or a stiff neck</li>
        <li>Pain or pressure behind the eye, or new blurred or double vision</li>
        <li>A headache that started after a blow to the head</li>
        <li>Headaches that are getting more frequent or severe despite 2–3 weeks of self-care</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-8"><h2 class="text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100 mb-3">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Where exactly is the Taiyang point?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Taiyang is in the hollow of the temple, about one finger width behind the midpoint between the outer tip of the eyebrow and the outer corner of the eye. Close your eyes and press your fingertips gently into the temple — the soft depression you find is the point.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">How long and how often should I massage Taiyang?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">One to two minutes of small, slow clockwise circles with the index and middle fingers is a good session, breathing deeply throughout. Use it at the first sign of a tension headache, during screen breaks, or as part of a wind-down before sleep. Gentle and consistent beats hard and rare.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can Taiyang help with eye strain?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes. Taiyang is classically used for tired, strained eyes, dry eyes, and visual fatigue after long screen use. Massage it with slow circles for 1–2 minutes, and pair it with Yintang and the site's other face points for a fuller screen-break routine.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Is it safe to massage Taiyang daily?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">For most people, gentle daily massage at the temples is safe — the key word is gentle, because the area sits close to the eye and the muscle layer is thin. Stop immediately and see a doctor if pressure causes dizziness, blurred vision, or a worsening headache.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="mb-3 rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">Can I press Taiyang at work?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">Yes — it is one of the most discreet points on the body. Rest your elbows on the desk, cup your temples with your fingertips, and work slow circles for 30–60 seconds. It looks like you are simply resting your head, and it is ideal during a long meeting or before a demanding task.</div>
    </details>
    <details class="rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-4 dark:border-stone-800 dark:bg-stone-900">
      <summary class="cursor-pointer font-semibold text-stone-900 dark:text-stone-100">When should I see a doctor instead of using Taiyang?</summary>
      <div class="mt-2 text-sm text-stone-500 dark:text-stone-400 leading-relaxed">See a doctor (or emergency care) for a sudden, severe, or "worst ever" headache; a headache with vision changes, confusion, or weakness; pain behind the eye; or a headache that follows a blow to the head. Frequent or worsening headaches despite 2–3 weeks of self-care also deserve a professional assessment.</div>
    </details>
    </section>

    <div class="mt-10 text-center">
      <a href="/points/" class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-sm text-stone-500 hover:text-forest-600 dark:text-stone-400 dark:hover:text-forest-300">Back to All Points</a>
    </div>
